  "narration": {
    "title": "Cleveland's Quiet Climb, San Diego's Loud One",
    "intro": "Two teams jumped this week in our power ranking. San Diego went 6-1 to slide into fifth, and Cleveland won five of six to leap five spots into ninth. Milwaukee stays on top, but the movement is all underneath.",
    "power_lead": "The Brewers hold first at 97-58 despite a modest 4-2 week, with Joey Ortiz going 8-for-22. The bigger stories are behind them: San Diego surged to fifth on a 6-1 run led by Ty France's 12-for-26, and Cleveland's five-win week, powered by Travis Bazzana's four homers, hauled the Guardians up five spots to ninth even at a plus-1 run differential for the year.",
    "offense_lead": "The Cubs lead the team-OPS board at .771, a hair ahead of the Dodgers at .764. Chicago's bats are broad rather than top-heavy, which is how you finish first in a category without one runaway name.",
    "staffs_lead": "The Yankees own the starter-ERA ranking at 3.25, and it shows in the box scores: Cam Schlittler struck out 14 over 12 innings this week. The Dodgers (3.45) and Rays (3.50) round out the top three, with New York also holding the best bullpen ERA at 3.09."
  }
